@page "/Docs"
@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<h3>A Blazor Server application that simulates the dynamics of a financial order book.</h3>

<h4>Parameters</h4>

<div class="mb-4">
    <strong>Speed</strong><br />
    Defines the frequency, in milliseconds, at which new orders are generated and submitted to the order book.
</div>

<div class="mb-4">
    <strong>Maximum Price</strong><br />
    Specifies the highest possible price that an automatically generated order can have.
</div>

<div class="mb-4">
    <strong>Maximum Amount</strong><br />
    Specifies the maximum quantity that an automatically generated order can contain.
</div>

<div class="mb-4">
    <strong>Optimism</strong><br />
    This value ranges between <code>0</code> and <code>1</code> and represents the overall market sentiment of traders.
    It determines the probability of a generated order being either a buy order or a sell order.

    <ul>
        <li>At an optimism value of <code>0.5</code>, buy and sell orders are generated with equal probability (50% each).</li>
        <li>At a value of <code>1</code>, only buy orders are generated.</li>
        <li>At a value of <code>0</code>, only sell orders are generated.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-4">
    <strong>Variance</strong><br />
    In real-world financial markets, orders are not placed completely randomly but rather around the current market price within a certain range.

    <br /><br />

    The variance parameter defines the maximum deviation from the current market price.
    For example, a variance value of <code>0.1</code> allows newly generated orders to deviate by up to <code>10%</code> from the current market price.
</div>

<div class="mb-4">
    <strong>Liquidity</strong><br />
    This value ranges between <code>0</code> and <code>1</code> and defines the probability of an order being created as either a limit order or a market order.

    <ul>
        <li>A liquidity value of <code>1</code> results in exclusively limit orders being generated.</li>
        <li>Lower liquidity values increase the likelihood of market orders being placed.</li>
    </ul>
</div>



